==Problem 11==

A <imath>\textit{tetromino}</imath> consists of four squares connected along their edges. There are five possible tetromino shapes, <imath>I</imath>, <imath>O</imath>, <imath>L</imath>, <imath>T</imath>, and <imath>S</imath>, shown below, which can be rotated or flipped over. Three tetrominoes are used to completely cover a <imath>3\times4</imath> rectangle. At least one of the tiles is an <imath>S</imath> tile. What are the other two tiles?

<asy>

unitsize(12);

add(grid(1,4));
label("I", (0.5,-1));

add(shift((5,0)) * grid(2,2));
label("O", (6,-1));

add(shift((11,0)) * grid(1,3));
add(shift((11,0)) * grid(2,1));
label("L", (12,-1));

add(shift((18,0)) * grid(1,1));
add(shift((17,1)) * grid(3,1));
label("T", (18.5,-1));

add(shift((25,1)) * grid(2,1));
add(shift((24,0)) * grid(2,1));
label("S", (25.5,-1));

add(shift((12,-6)) * grid(4,3));

